    <DataNode elementId="PALMITALDEHYDE" textLabel="hexadecanal" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="NIOYUNMRJMEDGI-UHFFFAOYSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">|FRAME: PALMITALDEHYDE Hexadecanal| (palmitaldehyde) is a volatile long-chain aliphatic aldehyde. In mammals the molecule serves as a social chemosignal detected by the OR37B receptor. This was first identified in mice, where it acts as a social-buffering agent |CITS: [25619114]|, and later observed in humans, where it blocks aggression in men but triggers aggression in women |CITS: [Mishor21]|.</Comment>
